Layer 0 — Portal Ingestion
This is your front door. SUGAM and MD Online both have APIs — you write adapter classes that translate their submission formats into your own internal format. Think of it like a universal plug. Whatever comes in — PDF, XML, DOCX, CSV — gets converted to one standard structure before anything else touches it.
The critical thing here is that ingestion must be instant. The portal submits a file, your system accepts it in under a second and queues it for processing. Never make the portal wait for AI to run. Use a background task queue for that.
Also store a SHA-256 checksum of every file the moment it arrives. This is how you detect duplicate uploads before wasting any compute, and it becomes evidence of document integrity for the regulator.

Layer 1 — Pre-processing Engine
Think of this as your document refinery. Raw PDFs come in — scanned, searchable, hand-annotated, multi-column — and clean structured text comes out. The key tools here are PyMuPDF for extracting native text from digital PDFs and Tesseract for scanned pages. Run native extraction first, fall back to OCR only when the page has no selectable text.
The smarter part is chunking. Don't just split text every 500 tokens — that destroys document structure. Split on section headings and logical boundaries so each chunk is a self-contained piece of meaning. This makes everything downstream — summarisation, retrieval, comparison — dramatically more accurate.
After chunking, generate embeddings for every chunk using a sentence-transformer model. Store these in ChromaDB. This is what powers semantic search, duplicate detection, and RAG-based summarisation later.

Layer 2 — Message Bus (Kafka)
This is what separates a platform from a script. Instead of chaining modules together in one big function call, every module publishes an event when it finishes and subscribes to events from upstream. The anonymiser listens for "document preprocessed." The summariser listens for "document anonymised." And so on.
The benefit is that if your SAE classifier crashes at 2am, it doesn't lose any documents — Kafka held them in the queue. When it restarts, it picks up exactly where it left off. You also get independent scaling — if summarisation is slow, spin up more summariser instances without touching anything else.
Always add a dead letter queue for each topic. When a message fails processing three times, it goes to the DLQ for manual inspection instead of blocking the whole pipeline.

Layer 3 — The Four AI Modules
Anonymisation — Don't build this from scratch. Microsoft's open-source Presidio library was built exactly for PII detection and is battle-tested. Your job is to extend it with custom recognizers for Indian-specific identifiers: Aadhaar number patterns, CIN numbers, Indian phone formats. Add spaCy's large English model on top for name and location detection. The output should not just redact — it should use consistent pseudonyms so the document stays readable. "Dr. <PERSON_1> referred patient to <HOSPITAL_1>" is far more useful to a reviewer than blank boxes everywhere.
Summarisation — The LLM call itself is the easy part. The hard part is making the output always structured and consistent. Define a strict Pydantic schema for each document type — drug application, SAE narrative, device submission — and force the LLM to return JSON matching that schema every single time. Validate the output. If it doesn't match, retry with a correction prompt. For very large documents, use a map-reduce pattern: summarise each section with a fast model, then synthesise the partial summaries with a powerful model.
Classification — Two separate problems. Completeness is a rule engine — you define what fields are required for each form type and check whether they're present. It's deterministic, not AI. Severity classification is a fine-tuned BERT model trained on SAE narratives labelled with CDSCO's own severity categories. The fine-tuning is what makes this production-grade — a generic LLM doesn't know the specific definitions of "life-threatening" versus "serious" under Schedule Y of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act. Duplicate detection uses FAISS — compare embeddings of incoming submissions against all previously stored ones and flag anything above a similarity threshold.
Comparison — Two-pass approach. First pass is a deterministic text diff that finds exactly what lines changed between document versions. Second pass sends those changes to an LLM and asks it to explain the regulatory significance — "the applicant removed contraindication for renal impairment" is far more useful than a raw diff. For inspection report generation, give the LLM a CDSCO-standard report template and let it fill in structured observations.

Layer 4 — Compliance and Governance
Every AI output passes through this layer before being stored or shown to anyone. It checks three things: did the anonymiser miss any PII in its own output, does the classification follow CDSCO mandatory review rules (death and life-threatening cases always require a human), and are all data handling practices consistent with DPDP Act 2023 and ICMR guidelines.
The most important thing to build here is the immutable audit log. Every AI decision — what went in, what came out, what model was used, what confidence score it had — is written to a chain-hashed log. Each entry contains the hash of the previous entry. This means any tampering with historical records is mathematically detectable. CDSCO regulators will love this because it gives them complete accountability without depending on your team's word.

Layer 5 — Data Storage
Three different stores for three different purposes. PostgreSQL holds structured data — submission records, reviewer decisions, case status, user accounts. MinIO is your object store — every raw document goes here and is never mutated. ChromaDB holds all the vector embeddings for semantic search and duplicate detection.
The critical database design decision is that the audit log table should have update and delete permissions revoked at the database level for the application user. The application can only insert. Not even a bug in your code can modify historical records.

Layer 6 — Output and Delivery
The reviewer dashboard is a case management system, not a report viewer. Think of it like Jira for regulatory submissions. Each submission is a case. The AI fills in the analysis. The reviewer sees it, can override any AI conclusion, adds their notes, and makes a disposition decision. Every override is logged — this is how you build a feedback dataset to improve your models over time.
PDF report generation uses WeasyPrint — you define HTML templates for each CDSCO report format, inject the AI-generated content, and render to PDF. This is far more controllable than trying to generate PDFs programmatically.
The REST API for portal integration follows standard webhook patterns — when a case is processed, SUGAM and MD Online get a callback with the result so reviewers inside those portals can see the AI analysis without leaving their existing workflow.